PROFILES += [
 dict(slug="anne-adams-tufts", name="Anne Adams Tufts (1729&ndash;1813)", dates="1729&ndash;1813", town="Menotomy (Arlington) and Winter Hill, Charlestown (now Somerville), Mass.", born="1729", died="1813", era="The Revolution", line="Wife of Peter Tufts of Winter Hill",
  bio='<p>Anne (or Anna) Adams was born in Menotomy, the northwest precinct of Cambridge that is now Arlington, a daughter of Lieut. Joseph Adams and Rachel (Allen) Adams. On April 19, 1750, she married ' + WH + ', and the couple raised a large family on his farm on Winter Hill. Twenty-five years to the day later, the Lexington alarm called her husband out.</p>'
   + Q("Anna, b. in Menotomy, July 8, 1729; m. April 19, 1750, Peter Tufts, Jr., b. April 19, 1729, d. 4 March, 1791. She d. 17 Feb., 1813, age 84.", ADG(964), note=OCR)
   + Q("The 19th of April, as it chanced, was the silver wedding anniversary of Peter and Anne Tufts, who lived at Winter Hill.", CHS(3, 99))
   + '<p>Her sister Mary married Peter&rsquo;s brother Nathan, so that, as Booth put it, &ldquo;The elder brother married the younger sister, the younger brother the elder sister.&rdquo; Booth describes the two women as being &ldquo;of strong character and great natural vigor of constitution&rdquo; (<em>Historic Leaves</em>, vol. 1, p. 21).</p>'
   + '<h3>Bunker Hill and the Winter Hill prisoners</h3>'
   + Q("After the battle of Bunker Hill, Anne Tufts assisted in binding up the wounds of eight wounded soldiers who were brought to her house; and later in the war when a part of Burgoyne's army was encamped as prisoners on Winter hill, she went to the camp and nursed all night the dying wife of one of the prisoners. Years afterward that soldier journeyed from Canada, where he had settled after the war, and sought out Mrs. Tufts to thank her again for that service and to ask her to point out the spot of his wife's grave.", BTH("21&ndash;22"))
   + '<p>The Massachusetts DAR history of 1905, drawing on an obituary &ldquo;written by a grandson,&rdquo; tells how she dressed the wounds:</p>'
   + Q("Anne Adams Tufts, with true motherly spirit, in this emergency, drew from her hospital supplies in the form of home-spun linen sheets and spreading them with the cool sweet cream from her dairy, dressed the wounds of the soldiers, asking not whether they be friend or foe.", MDAR, note=OCR)
   + Q("The first school on Winter Hill &ldquo;was kept&rdquo; in the kitchen of this house, and later, when a schoolhouse was built, the first printing office was established here by a descendant.", MDAR, note="(The printer was her grandson Edmund Tufts; see Booth, p. 24.)")
   + '<p>Her husband served as a Charlestown selectman in 1781 and died in 1791; Anne lived on as a widow for twenty-two years (Booth, p. 21). The house stood &ldquo;near the westerly corner of Central street and Broadway&rdquo; before it was moved to Lowell Street (Booth, p. 21). Its door-stone remains in Paul Revere Park, Broadway at Main Street, under the 1909 DAR tablet; see <a href="{P}generations/colonial.html#anne-adams">the tablet and its transcription</a>.</p>'
   + '<h3>The chapter named for her</h3>'
   + Q("At a meeting held March 16, it was voted that the Chapter be named for Anne Adams Tufts, a resident, in the days of the Revolution, of Winter Hill, Charlestown (now Somerville).", MDAR, note=OCR)
   + '<p>The chapter grew from an informal meeting on March 10, 1897, at the home of Miss Mary A. Bradford, &ldquo;a lineal descendant of Governor Bradford,&rdquo; who became the first regent. It was approved by the National Society on January 6, 1898, with fifteen charter members; its seal was given February 17, 1899, and the charter presented in May 1899 by State Regent Sara W. Daggett. Anne&rsquo;s great-granddaughter Mrs. Helen Magoun Heald served it &ldquo;continuously since its inception, as Registrar, Regent and Historian&rdquo; (Mass. DAR history, 1905). A Somerville history blog adds that the chapter&rsquo;s charter was framed in wood from an apple tree on the Adams&ndash;Magoun estate, from which its gavel was also made (' + BOUSE + ').</p>',
  service='<p>Civilian service: nursing the wounded after Bunker Hill (June 17, 1775) and a dying woman of the Convention Army camp on Winter Hill (1777&ndash;78), as told by Booth (1902) and the Mass. DAR history (1905). No contemporary record of this service has been found.</p>',
  family='<p>Father: Lieut. Joseph Adams of Menotomy. Mother: Rachel (Allen) Adams (Adams genealogy, p. 964). Brother: Capt. William Adams (1725&ndash;1787) of Menotomy, a captain in Col. Thatcher&rsquo;s regiment. Sister Mary (b. 1733) married Nathan Tufts, Peter&rsquo;s brother, in 1751; sister Rebecca married Samuel Kent (Adams, p. 964; <em>Historic Leaves</em>, vol. 3, p. 89).</p>'
   + Q("i. Peter Tufts, b. May 20, 1751, d. 19 March, 1752. ii. Peter Tufts 3d, b. Jan. 9, 1753; m. July 2, 1773, Hannah Adams &hellip; iii. John Tufts, b. Nov. 24, 1754; m. Elizabeth Perry. iv. Ann Tufts, b. Jan. 2, 1757; m. Abel Richardson. v. Elizabeth Tufts, b. Oct. 23, 1758; m. March 2, 1787, Daniel Swan. vi. Joseph Tufts, b. July 12, 1760; m. Abigail Tufts. vii. Lydia Tufts, b. June 10, 1762; m. Rev. Robert Gray of Dover, N. H. viii. Asa Tufts, b. July 2, 1764, d. 24 Oct., 1828. ix. Thomas Tufts, b. May 18, 1766 &hellip; x. Lucy Tufts, b. Nov. 12, 1767; m. Jacob Osgood. xi. Rebecca Tufts, b. Sept. 20, 1769; m. Nathan Adams, Esq. &hellip; xii. Sarah Tufts &hellip; m. Feb. 25, 1802, Maj. Joseph Adams", ADG(973), note=OCR)
   + '<p>Children with profiles: ' + PL("peter-tufts-jr", "Peter Tufts Jr. (1753&ndash;1832)") + ' and ' + PL("john-tufts-1754", "John Tufts (b. 1754)") + '. Grandson: ' + PL("peter-tufts-1774", "Peter Tufts the surveyor (1774&ndash;1825)") + '. Her granddaughter Sarah Ann Adams (b. 1802), daughter of Sarah Tufts and Joseph Adams, married John C. Magoun (' + BOUSE + ').</p>',
  unverified="<strong>Birth place:</strong> Menotomy (Adams, p. 964; the 1905 DAR history pictures her &ldquo;birthplace&rdquo; in Menotomy). Earlier pages of this site quote Jones (&ldquo;Anne Adams of Quincy&rdquo;) and the family history (&ldquo;daughter of Joseph Adams of Cambridge&rdquo;); Menotomy was then part of Cambridge, so the family history agrees and Jones appears to be wrong. <strong>Death:</strong> 17 Feb. 1813 (Adams, p. 964; Jones, p. 129) against Feb. 7 (Wyman, p. 965). <strong>Husband&rsquo;s birth:</strong> Adams gives April 19, 1729; NEHGR 51 (p. 304) gives 24 April 1728. <strong>Number of wounded:</strong> Booth says eight soldiers; the 1905 DAR history says the house was &ldquo;turned into a hospital.&rdquo; <strong>Helen Magoun Heald:</strong> the 2015 blog calls her the chapter&rsquo;s founding regent; the 1905 DAR history names Mary A. Bradford first regent and Mrs. Heald second regent. The site follows the 1905 history. <strong>The 1909 Boston Globe clipping</strong> supplied for this page (newspapers.com clipping 98669433) could not be opened from this machine. Note: Pending further work."),
 dict(slug="peter-tufts-1774", name="Peter Tufts the Surveyor (1774&ndash;1825)", dates="1774&ndash;1825", town="Medford, Charlestown (Somerville) and Cambridgeport, Mass.", born="1774", died="1825", era="19th Century", line="Grandson of Anne Adams Tufts",
  bio='<p>Among the many Peters of the Winter Hill family, this one was known as &ldquo;Peter, the surveyor.&rdquo; A son of ' + PL("peter-tufts-jr", "Peter Tufts Jr. (1753&ndash;1832)") + ' and Hannah Adams, and a grandson of ' + PL("anne-adams-tufts", "Anne Adams Tufts") + ', he was born in 1774, probably on the Royal farm in Medford where his father had settled and where, Booth says, all his father&rsquo;s children were born.</p>'
   + Q("Peter Tufts, Jr., lived a life of great activity. He was keeper of the Powder House, and when in 1815 the powder was transferred to the new storehouse at the end of Magazine street, Cambridgeport, he continued as keeper, took up his residence near the magazine and died there in 1825. Mr. Tufts was a civil engineer by profession, and among the many Peters is designated as &lsquo;Peter, the surveyor.&rsquo; He drew a plan of Charlestown in 1818, and the mass of plans that he left behind him shows how laboriously he was engaged in the surveys of public and private property.", BTH("22&ndash;23"))
   + Q("In public life he was prominent, having been trustee of schools, selectman for most of the years between 1806 and 1817, assessor for several terms and representative to the General Court for six terms, between the years 1809 and 1819.", BTH(23))
   + '<h3>His surveys</h3><p>At twenty-three he drew a manuscript road survey, signed &ldquo;By Peter Tufts junior June 14th 1797,&rdquo; of the roughly eighteen-mile road from Concord meeting house through Lexington and Menotomy to Cambridge and the new West Boston Bridge, the route the British marched on April 19, 1775. It is drawn in ink and watercolor at half a mile to the inch, with a compass rose (on which, the dealer notes, East and West are reversed) and the older road via Waltham copied &ldquo;from a old Plan Drawn by Mr Osburn.&rdquo; The dealer reports that library catalogs list some twenty-one manuscript maps and plans by Tufts dated 1793 to 1823 (' + BRM97 + '). The sheet has been sold and is not reproduced here.</p>'
   + '<p>His best-known work is the engraved <em>Plan of Charlestown Peninsula</em> (1818), &ldquo;from accurate survey by Peter Tufts Jun<sup>r</sup>. Esq<sup>r</sup>.&rdquo; A 1794 manuscript plan of Medford signed &ldquo;Peter Tufts jun<sup>r</sup>&rdquo; may be his or his father&rsquo;s. Both are shown on <a href="{P}places.html#maps">Places: Maps</a>.</p>',
  service='<p>Civil: selectman (most years 1806&ndash;1817), assessor, school trustee, and representative to the General Court for six terms between 1809 and 1819; keeper of the Powder House and, from 1815, of the Cambridgeport magazine (Booth, p. 23). Too young for Revolutionary service.</p>',
  family='<p>Father: ' + PL("peter-tufts-jr", "Peter Tufts Jr. (1753&ndash;1832)") + '. Mother: Hannah Adams, niece of Anne Adams (Booth, p. 22). Grandparents: ' + WH + ' and ' + PL("anne-adams-tufts", "Anne Adams Tufts") + '. He had twelve children (Booth, p. 23).</p>',
  unverified="<strong>Exact birth and death dates</strong> have not been found; Booth gives only the years 1774 and 1825. <strong>The 1794 Medford plan:</strong> father or son (see Places: Maps). <strong>The 1797 survey&rsquo;s purpose</strong> is unknown; the dealer suggests the West Boston Bridge proprietors may have commissioned it, which is conjecture."),
 dict(slug="john-tufts-1754", name="John Tufts (b. 1754)", dates="b. 1754", town="Winter Hill, Charlestown (now Somerville), Mass.", born="1754", died="", era="The Revolution", line="Son of Peter and Anne (Adams) Tufts of Winter Hill",
  bio='<p>John Tufts, the second son of ' + WH + ' and ' + PL("anne-adams-tufts", "Anne Adams Tufts") + ', was a young man of about twenty on the morning his father rode out on Paul Revere&rsquo;s alarm. Ellen Chase, drawing on Jones&rsquo;s <em>Under Colonial Roofs</em>, tells what followed:</p>'
   + Q("Paul Revere's alarm took Peter to the front, his son John, a lad of eighteen, being charged to stay behind and take care of things. The boy, however, could not be pacified at home and followed on, up beyond Lexington. While crossing one of the hills he met Elizabeth Perry, crying and running from her blazing home. He guarded her to her relatives and three years later made her his bride.", CHS(3, 99))
   + Q("John Tufts, the second son of Peter of Winter Hill, was a scientific farmer and gardener. During the Revolution, his father established him on the farm the house of which is now rented by the Somerville Historical society.", BTH(23))
   + '<p>Booth calls that house &ldquo;the headquarters of General Lee&rdquo; (p. 23); it stayed in the family through his son Oliver. Elizabeth Perry was, Booth notes, a granddaughter of James Tufts of Medford. They had thirteen children, among them John Jr.; Benjamin, a milk farmer on Washington Street; Oliver, who kept the old Lee house until his death in 1883; Leonard, father of James W. Tufts, the maker of soda-water apparatus; and Asa (Booth, pp. 23&ndash;24).</p>',
  service='<p>The Lexington alarm story above is family tradition as retold by Chase (1910). No record of his service has yet been matched in <em>Massachusetts Soldiers and Sailors</em>.</p>',
  family='<p>Father: ' + WH + '. Mother: ' + PL("anne-adams-tufts", "Anne Adams Tufts") + '. Brother: ' + PL("peter-tufts-jr", "Peter Tufts Jr.") + '. Nephew: ' + PL("peter-tufts-1774", "Peter Tufts the surveyor") + '.</p>',
  unverified="<strong>Birth:</strong> Adams (p. 973) gives Nov. 24, 1754; Booth (p. 23) says 1755. Chase&rsquo;s &ldquo;a lad of eighteen&rdquo; does not fit either (he would have been twenty). <strong>Death:</strong> Note: Pending further work. <strong>Marriage date</strong> (&ldquo;three years later,&rdquo; i.e., about 1778): Note: Pending further work."),
]
