# Further details for existing profiles
NS = {
 "peter-tufts-jr": '<p>Booth adds the outline of his life after the war:</p>'
   + Q("Peter, the eldest son of Peter and Anne (Adams) Tufts, was born in the old house on Winter hill in 1753. He married Hannah Adams, a niece of Anne Adams. He settled in early life on the Royal farm in Medford on the site of the present trottingpark, and here all of his children were born. It is related that Peter was one of the party that fortified Dorchester Heights, which compelled the evacuation of Boston. Such precautions were observed that the wheels of the wagons were muffled, and the men themselves were in their stocking feet.", BTH(22))
   + Q("In 1788, Peter bought of his cousin, Daniel Tufts, the farm opposite the Powder House, afterwards owned by Charles Tufts, and in 1806 built upon it the large three-storied mansion house taken down a few years ago. This house was within the limits of Medford till 1811, when, through the efforts of Mr. Tufts, a small triangular piece of land, including the house-lot at the corner of Broadway and Elm street, was set off to Charlestown.", BTH(22))
   + '<p>Of his eleven children, Peter (' + PL("peter-tufts-1774", "the surveyor") + ') and Joel stayed in Somerville; Thomas and Aaron settled in New York State; and his daughters Hannah and Anne married Samuel Tufts Jr. and Isaac Tufts (Booth, p. 22). The 1905 DAR history calls him &ldquo;the eldest son, Peter, jr.,&rdquo; and says he &ldquo;was a &lsquo;Minute Man&rsquo;&rdquo; (' + MDAR + ').</p>'
   + Q("ii. Peter Tufts 3d, b. Jan. 9, 1753; m. July 2, 1773, Hannah Adams, dau. of Dea. Joseph and Martha (Frost) Adams.", ADG(973), note=OCR)
   + UNV("Marriage date: Adams (p. 973) gives July 2, 1773; Wyman (p. 965) gives July 22, 1773. The Dorchester Heights story is family tradition (&ldquo;It is related&rdquo;)."),
 "timothy-tufts": '<p>Chase&rsquo;s account of April 19 adds two scenes at his house. In the small hours, as the British marched out toward Lexington:</p>'
   + Q("After this there was no dwelling until Timothy Tufts's was reached on Elm Street, near Willow Avenue. There the dog barked and Mrs. Tufts roused and saw from her bed the gun-barrels glistening in the moonlight. She woke her husband and they saw the soldiers halt and drink from their well. Then they turned into Beech Street and disappeared.", CHS(2, 355))
   + '<p>And on the retreat that evening:</p>'
   + Q("The troops here planted their cannon on the northerly spur of Spring Hill, behind Timothy Tufts' house, and won a short respite. &hellip; Leaving a few dead, now buried in Mr. Tufts' lot, just inside the fence, the troops crowded on down Elm Street into Somerville Avenue.", CHS(3, 166))
   + '<p>His wife was a niece of his sisters-in-law: the Somerville Historical Society notes that of Deacon Joseph Adams&rsquo;s children &ldquo;Anna became the wife of Timothy Tufts, another brother of Peter, Jr., and Hannah married Peter Tufts, the third&rdquo; (' + HL3 + '). See ' + PL("anne-adams-tufts", "Anne Adams Tufts") + '.</p>'
   + Q("Samuel Tufts occupied an old gambrel-roofed house, later General Green's headquarters, on the west side of the road, near Laurel Street. He was running bullets with his negro, in a small hut back of the house, and did not hear the Regulars, but Widow Elizabeth Rand &hellip; is said to have run in her night-clothes to tell Tufts. He instantly saddled his horse and galloped across his farm to Cambridge with the news", CHS(2, 355), note="(Samuel Tufts of Milk Row, Timothy&rsquo;s brother, b. 1737.)"),
 "simon-tufts-11-1": Q("He was carried into Cooper's tavern, then in use as a hospital, and Dr. Simon Tufts of Medford, &ldquo;a perfect Chesterfield,&rdquo; at first feared to dress his wounds. Doctors Welch and Spring, however, finally ventured to care for his gun-shot wound and some thirteen bayonet holes.", CHS(3, 147), note="(The patient was Samuel Whittemore of Menotomy, wounded on April 19, 1775. Chase cites the <em>Medford Historical Register</em>, October 1898.)")
   + Q("He was an indented apprentice to Simon Tufts, M. D., at the age of fourteen, until he became of age.", LOR, note="(Of Gov. John Brooks, born at Medford June 6, 1752.)")
   + Q("September 14th Mr. Samuel Tufts, of Medford, advertised that a basket of crockery ware had been taken up in that town, some time after the battle, which the owner might reclaim.", CHS(3, 268), note="(A Medford Samuel Tufts in 1775; which Samuel is not stated.)"),
 "peter-tufts-1617": '<p>The Malden bicentennial history of 1850 gives a compact early account:</p>'
   + Q("He kept the &ldquo;Penny Ferry&rdquo; where Malden Bridge now stands, in 1646. In 1654 he is registered as a Juryman in the Court Record books of Middlesex county. In 1664, he bought of Mrs. Nowell, executrix of Increase Nowell, two hundred acres of land in Charlestown, at a place called Mystic-fields.", MAL(249), note=OCR)
   + Q("Peter Tufts, Sen., was Tythingman or Overseer, to look after the morals of the district of Malden. The will of Mr. T. is dated March 1, 1693, at which time he was 74 years of age. He died May 13, 1700, aged 83, and lies buried in the old Malden burying-ground. His grave-stones, made probably in England, are still in good preservation.", MAL(250), note=OCR)
   + '<p>He was among the Malden men who signed, &ldquo;in the name of the rest,&rdquo; the town&rsquo;s 1662 petition to the General Court for a grant of land at Pennacook (Mass. Archives, vol. 112, p. 147, printed in the same book, p. 59). The book also suggests that Malden &ldquo;might have received its present name from him,&rdquo; which is speculation.</p>'
   + UNV("The Malden book&rsquo;s claims that he came &ldquo;between 1638 and 1640&rdquo; from Malden in England, and that the town was named for him, are inferences without a cited record. It gives his wife&rsquo;s birth as 1628 and death as 1703, aged 75."),
 "peter-tufts-1648": Q("Elizabeth married Peter Tufts of Charlestown, Aug. 26, 1670, and died July 15, 1685, in the 33d year of her age.", MAL(233), note="(Of Elizabeth Lynde, daughter of Thomas Lynde of Malden.)")
   + Q("The children of Peter were Peter, afterwards known as Capt. Peter of Medford, and who lies buried in that town", MAL(250))
   + UNV("Elizabeth Lynde&rsquo;s death year: the Malden book gives July 15, 1685; NEHGR gives 15 June 1684 and Wyman and Brooks July 15, 1684. The Malden book&rsquo;s statement that Capt. Peter &ldquo;lies buried&rdquo; in Medford agrees with the Medford vital records (p. 445) against Brooks."),
}
_EXTRA_OS = EXTRA
def EXTRA(path):
    out = _EXTRA_OS(path)
    s = path[9:-5] if path.startswith("profiles/") else ""
    if s in NS:
        out += '{DIV}<section id="more-sources"><h2>Further Details from Additional Sources</h2>' + NS[s] + '</section>'
    if s == "peter-tufts-jr":
        out = out.replace("His son Peter (1774&ndash;1825), &ldquo;Peter, the surveyor,&rdquo;", 'His son <a href="{P}profiles/peter-tufts-1774.html">Peter (1774&ndash;1825)</a>, &ldquo;Peter, the surveyor,&rdquo;')
    if path == "places.html":
        out += ('{DIV}<section id="more-maps"><h2>More Maps and Plans</h2>'
          '<h3>Road from Concord to Cambridge, surveyed by Peter Tufts Jr. (1797)</h3><p>A manuscript survey &ldquo;By Peter Tufts junior June 14th 1797&rdquo; of the road the British took on April 19, 1775, from Concord meeting house through Lexington and Menotomy to the West Boston Bridge. Its maker was <a href="{P}profiles/peter-tufts-1774.html">Peter Tufts the surveyor (1774&ndash;1825)</a>, grandson of Anne Adams Tufts. The sheet is privately held and is linked, not reproduced: ' + BRM97 + '.</p>'
          '<h3>Siege of Boston plan (1776)</h3><p><em>A plan of the town of Boston and its environs, with the lines, batteries, and incampments of the British and American armies</em>, attributed to Sir Thomas Hyde Page, manuscript, [1776], Library of Congress. It shows the siege lines that ran across the Charlestown mainland where the Winter Hill and Milk Row Tufts farms lay. <a href="https://collections.leventhalmap.org/search/commonwealth:z603vj67s">Leventhal Map Center (georeferenced)</a>; licensed CC BY-NC-SA, so it is linked rather than shown.</p></section>'
          '{DIV}<section id="tufts-subdivisions"><h2>The Tufts Subdivisions of South Medford (Winter Hill)</h2>'
          '<p>Much of the Winter Hill neighborhood of South Medford was Tufts land in the nineteenth century. <a href="{P}profiles/nathan-tufts-1764.html">Nathan Tufts (c. 1764&ndash;1835)</a>, a Charlestown butcher and tanner and a son of Nathan and Mary (Adams) Tufts, bought large holdings, including the Dexter farm, and left them to his nephews. Sawyer records his death in October 1835, aged seventy-one; a later inventory form gives 1839.</p>'
          + Q("Nathan Tufts (1764-1839) was a Charlestown butcher and tanner who purchased significant acreage in the two neighboring towns, valued at nearly $120,000 at the time of his death; of this, 630 acres were in six Medford holdings, valued at $51,000.", MHC)
          + '<p>Amos Tufts&rsquo;s sons William (1802&ndash;1862) and Edward (1806&ndash;1875) and grandsons George F. Tufts (1825&ndash;1920), a civil engineer and later bank president, and Alfred Tufts (1829&ndash;1882), who surveyed the plans, laid the land out in subdivisions in 1854 and 1861. Joseph, Edward and Henry Streets &ldquo;were named for members of the Tufts family, Dexter after the earlier owner.&rdquo; George Tufts reorganized part of section 5 in 1880, and the six end houses of Edward Street were built by 1889 (' + MHC + ').</p></section>')
    return out
_page_os = page
def page(path, title, kicker, h1, sub, body, *a, **k):
    if path == "generations/colonial.html":
        body = body.replace('<section id="anne-adams"><h2>Anne Adams Tufts</h2>', '<section id="anne-adams"><h2>Anne Adams Tufts</h2>\n<p><strong>Full profile:</strong> <a href="{P}profiles/anne-adams-tufts.html">Anne Adams Tufts (1729&ndash;1813)</a>, with her birth in Menotomy, her children, and the founding of the chapter named for her.</p>', 1)
        body = body.replace('<p>In 1909 the chapter set a bronze tablet', '<p>The 1905 history of the Massachusetts DAR records that the chapter grew from a meeting at the home of Miss Mary A. Bradford, its first regent, and that it was named for Anne on March 16, 1897. Mrs. Heald was Helen Magoun Heald, Anne&rsquo;s great-granddaughter, the chapter&rsquo;s second regent (' + MDAR + ').</p>\n<p>In 1909 the chapter set a bronze tablet', 1)
        body = body.replace("Mrs. Heald's full name and term as regent. The chapter's founders, DAR chapter number,", "Mrs. Heald's dates as regent. The DAR chapter number,")
        body = body.replace("Anne's death date: Wyman", "Anne's birthplace: Menotomy, per the Adams genealogy (1898), p. 964. Anne's death date: Wyman")
    return _page_os(path, title, kicker, h1, sub, body, *a, **k)
